   "grounded": false,
   "answer": "imagine options dealers are running a giant floating bridge. when they sell people calls or puts, they have to buy or sell the underlying stock to stay balanced so they don't get wiped out. \n\ngamma is how fast that balancing act gets completely deranged when the market moves. \n\nwhen dealers are sitting on a massive pile of dealer-long options (positive gamma), they have to sell into every rally and buy every dip like boring little dampeners, pinning the market in place and killing volatility. \n\nwhen dealers are short options (negative gamma), it\u2019s the exact opposite: the market moves an inch, they have to panic-buy or panic-sell to catch up, turning a small move into an absolute face-ripping rocket or a straight-down waterfall."
  },
